  from jsonb_array_elements('[{"n":51,"t":"assess_parts","p":"a A sequence is defined by u₁ = 2 and uₙ₊₁ = 3uₙ. Write down the first five terms of the sequence.\\n\\nb Describe in words how each term is obtained from the previous term, and state whether the sequence is linear, quadratic or geometric. Give a reason for your answer.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"a A sequence is defined by u₁ = 2 and uₙ₊₁ = 3uₙ. Write down the first five terms of the sequence."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"b Describe in words how each term is obtained from the previous term, and state whether the sequence is linear, quadratic or geometric. Give a reason for your answer."}]}]},"e":"Model answer — (a) u₁ = 2, u₂ = 3 × 2 = 6, u₃ = 3 × 6 = 18, u₄ = 3 × 18 = 54, u₅ = 3 × 54 = 162. (b) Each term is obtained by multiplying the previous term by 3. The sequence is GEOMETRIC because the ratio between consecutive terms is constant (equal to 3) — the differences 4, 12, 36, 108 are not constant (not linear), and neither are the second differences (not quadratic).\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: all five terms correct (award 1 for the first three correct). (b) 2 marks: 1 for ‘multiply by 3’ / recursive description; 1 for ‘geometric’ with a reason (constant ratio 3, vs constant difference for linear).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — terms in any separators; ‘geometric with ratio 3’; ‘uₙ₊₁ = 3uₙ’.","m":4,"df":"easy","tp":"Recursive and Explicit Formulas","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":51,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Feed 2 into 3×, again and again.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a) first five terms","prompt":"Write down the first five terms of the sequence.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Start at 2 and multiply by 3 each time.","Multiply by 3 each time — four multiplications from the start to the fifth term."],"accepted":["2, 6, 18, 54, 162","2,6,18,54,162","2 6 18 54 162"],"keywords":[["2"],["6"],["18"],["54"],["162"]],"minKeywordGroups":5},{"id":"b","label":"(b) describe and classify","prompt":"Describe in words how each term is obtained from the previous term, and state whether the sequence is linear, quadratic or geometric. Give a reason.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Each term = previous term × 3 — a constant multiplier, not a constant difference.","A constant multiplier each step — is this addition or multiplication?"],"accepted":["Each term is 3 times the previous term; it is geometric because the ratio between consecutive terms is constantly 3 (not a constant difference or second difference).","Multiply by 3 each step; geometric, common ratio 3."],"keywords":[["3","times","multiply","×"],["geometric"],["ratio","constant","each"]],"minKeywordGroups":2}],"marking":"Model answer — (a) u₁ = 2, u₂ = 3 × 2 = 6, u₃ = 3 × 6 = 18, u₄ = 3 × 18 = 54, u₅ = 3 × 54 = 162. (b) Each term is obtained by multiplying the previous term by 3. The sequence is GEOMETRIC because the ratio between consecutive terms is constant (equal to 3) — the differences 4, 12, 36, 108 are not constant (not linear), and neither are the second differences (not quadratic).\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: all five terms correct (award 1 for the first three correct). (b) 2 marks: 1 for ‘multiply by 3’ / recursive description; 1 for ‘geometric’ with a reason (constant ratio 3, vs constant difference for linear).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — terms in any separators; ‘geometric with ratio 3’; ‘uₙ₊₁ = 3uₙ’.","marking_criteria":"Model answer — (a) u₁ = 2, u₂ = 3 × 2 = 6, u₃ = 3 × 6 = 18, u₄ = 3 × 18 = 54, u₅ = 3 × 54 = 162. (b) Each term is obtained by multiplying the previous term by 3. The sequence is GEOMETRIC because the ratio between consecutive terms is constant (equal to 3) — the differences 4, 12, 36, 108 are not constant (not linear), and neither are the second differences (not quadratic).\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: all five terms correct (award 1 for the first three correct). (b) 2 marks: 1 for ‘multiply by 3’ / recursive description; 1 for ‘geometric’ with a reason (constant ratio 3, vs constant difference for linear).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — terms in any separators; ‘geometric with ratio 3’; ‘uₙ₊₁ = 3uₙ’.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"any","response_guidance":""},"marking":"Model answer — (a) u₁ = 2, u₂ = 3 × 2 = 6, u₃ = 3 × 6 = 18, u₄ = 3 × 18 = 54, u₅ = 3 × 54 = 162. (b) Each term is obtained by multiplying the previous term by 3. The sequence is GEOMETRIC because the ratio between consecutive terms is constant (equal to 3) — the differences 4, 12, 36, 108 are not constant (not linear), and neither are the second differences (not quadratic).\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: all five terms correct (award 1 for the first three correct). (b) 2 marks: 1 for ‘multiply by 3’ / recursive description; 1 for ‘geometric’ with a reason (constant ratio 3, vs constant difference for linear).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — terms in any separators; ‘geometric with ratio 3’; ‘uₙ₊₁ = 3uₙ’."},{"n":52,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A linear sequence has first term 7 and common difference 5. Write an explicit formula for the nth term, uₙ.\\n\\nb Use your formula to find u₁₀, showing your substitution clearly.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A linear sequence has first term 7 and common difference 5. Write an explicit formula for the nth term, uₙ."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"b Use your formula to find u₁₀, showing your substitution clearly."}]}]},"e":"Model answer — (a) uₙ = 7 + (n − 1) × 5 = 7 + 5n − 5 = 5n + 2. (b) u₁₀ = 5 × 10 + 2 = 50 + 2 = 52 (equivalently 7 + 9 × 5 = 52). Showing the substitution: u₁₀ = 7 + (10 − 1) × 5 = 7 + 45 = 52.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for using uₙ = 7 + (n − 1)·5 with correct substitution of d = 5 and first term 7, 1 for the simplified uₙ = 5n + 2. Accept unsimplified uₙ = 7 + 5(n − 1). (b) 2 marks: 1 for the substitution shown (n = 10 into either form), 1 for u₁₀ = 52.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘uₙ = 5n + 2’, ‘uₙ = 7 + 5(n − 1)’; u₁₀ with any substitution shown.","m":4,"df":"medium","tp":"Recursive and Explicit Formulas","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":52,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"uₙ = first term + (n − 1) × difference.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a) explicit formula","prompt":"Write an explicit formula for the nth term, uₙ.","kind":"expression","points":2,"hints":["uₙ = first term + (n − 1) × common difference.","Substitute the first term and the difference, then expand."],"accepted":["uₙ = 5n + 2","un = 5n+2","5n+2","uₙ = 7 + 5(n − 1)","7 + (n-1)*5","u(n) = 5n + 2","5n + 2"]},{"id":"b","label":"(b) u₁₀","prompt":"Use your formula to find u₁₀, showing your substitution clearly.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Substitute n = 10 into your formula.","Evaluate your own formula at n = 10."],"answer":52,"tolerance":0}],"marking":"Model answer — (a) uₙ = 7 + (n − 1) × 5 = 7 + 5n − 5 = 5n + 2. (b) u₁₀ = 5 × 10 + 2 = 50 + 2 = 52 (equivalently 7 + 9 × 5 = 52). Showing the substitution: u₁₀ = 7 + (10 − 1) × 5 = 7 + 45 = 52.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for using uₙ = 7 + (n − 1)·5 with correct substitution of d = 5 and first term 7, 1 for the simplified uₙ = 5n + 2. Accept unsimplified uₙ = 7 + 5(n − 1). (b) 2 marks: 1 for the substitution shown (n = 10 into either form), 1 for u₁₀ = 52.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘uₙ = 5n + 2’, ‘uₙ = 7 + 5(n − 1)’; u₁₀ with any substitution shown.","marking_criteria":"Model answer — (a) uₙ = 7 + (n − 1) × 5 = 7 + 5n − 5 = 5n + 2. (b) u₁₀ = 5 × 10 + 2 = 50 + 2 = 52 (equivalently 7 + 9 × 5 = 52). Showing the substitution: u₁₀ = 7 + (10 − 1) × 5 = 7 + 45 = 52.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for using uₙ = 7 + (n − 1)·5 with correct substitution of d = 5 and first term 7, 1 for the simplified uₙ = 5n + 2. Accept unsimplified uₙ = 7 + 5(n − 1). (b) 2 marks: 1 for the substitution shown (n = 10 into either form), 1 for u₁₀ = 52.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘uₙ = 5n + 2’, ‘uₙ = 7 + 5(n − 1)’; u₁₀ with any substitution shown.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"any","response_guidance":""},"marking":"Model answer — (a) uₙ = 7 + (n − 1) × 5 = 7 + 5n − 5 = 5n + 2. (b) u₁₀ = 5 × 10 + 2 = 50 + 2 = 52 (equivalently 7 + 9 × 5 = 52). Showing the substitution: u₁₀ = 7 + (10 − 1) × 5 = 7 + 45 = 52.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for using uₙ = 7 + (n − 1)·5 with correct substitution of d = 5 and first term 7, 1 for the simplified uₙ = 5n + 2. Accept unsimplified uₙ = 7 + 5(n − 1). (b) 2 marks: 1 for the substitution shown (n = 10 into either form), 1 for u₁₀ = 52.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘uₙ = 5n + 2’, ‘uₙ = 7 + 5(n − 1)’; u₁₀ with any substitution shown."},{"n":53,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A student writes that the sequence 2, 6, 18, 54, … has nth term uₙ = 2 × 3ⁿ. Test the student''s rule at n = 1 and at n = 2, and explain the error.\\n\\nb Write a correct explicit rule for the sequence, and check that it produces all four terms given.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A student writes that the sequence 2, 6, 18, 54, … has nth term uₙ = 2 × 3ⁿ. Test the student''s rule at n = 1 and at n = 2, and explain the error."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"b Write a correct explicit rule for the sequence, and check that it produces all four terms given."}]}]},"e":"Model answer — (a) n = 1: 2 × 3¹ = 6, but the first term is 2. n = 2: 2 × 3² = 18, but the second term is 6. The student’s rule gives u₁ = 6 — it generates the sequence shifted by one term (it counts n factors of 3 starting from the first term instead of n − 1). (b) Correct rule: uₙ = 2 × 3ⁿ⁻¹. Check: n = 1 → 2 × 3⁰ = 2 ✓; n = 2 → 2 × 3¹ = 6 ✓; n = 3 → 2 × 3² = 18 ✓; n = 4 → 2 × 3³ = 54 ✓.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for both substitutions shown (6 and 18), 1 for explaining the shift (exponent should be n − 1 / rule gives first term 6). (b) 2 marks: 1 for the correct rule uₙ = 2 × 3ⁿ⁻¹ (equivalent forms accepted), 1 for the four checks (all four terms verified).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘uₙ = 2·3ⁿ⁻¹’; checks in any order; error phrased as ‘exponent starts at n = 1 instead of 0’.","m":4,"df":"medium","tp":"Recursive and Explicit Formulas","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":53,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","next_time_hint":"Plug n = 1 into the student’s rule — what do you get?","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a) test the rule","prompt":"Test the student''s rule at n = 1 and at n = 2, and explain the error.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Test the student’s rule at n = 1 and n = 2 against the printed first two terms.","The student’s rule makes the first term 6 — one power of 3 too many."],"accepted":["n=1 gives 6 (≠ 2) and n=2 gives 18 (≠ 6); the exponent is shifted — the first term is 6, not 2","at n = 1, u₁ = 6 instead of 2; at n = 2, u₂ = 18 instead of 6; the exponent should be n − 1, not n"],"keywords":[["6"],["18"],["n - 1","n−1","shift","one too many","first term","3¹ instead"]],"minKeywordGroups":2},{"id":"b","label":"(b) correct rule and check","prompt":"Write a correct explicit rule for the sequence, and check that it produces all four terms given.","kind":"expression","points":2,"hints":["2 is 3 to the power 0 — which exponent makes the rule produce 2 at n = 1?","Adjust the exponent so the rule yields 2 for n = 1."],"accepted":["uₙ = 2 × 3^(n-1)","un = 2*3^(n-1)","2*3^(n-1)","uₙ = 2 × 3ⁿ⁻¹","uₙ = 2·3ⁿ⁻¹","2 × 3ⁿ⁻¹","u(n) = 2*3^(n-1)"]}],"marking":"Model answer — (a) n = 1: 2 × 3¹ = 6, but the first term is 2. n = 2: 2 × 3² = 18, but the second term is 6. The student’s rule gives u₁ = 6 — it generates the sequence shifted by one term (it counts n factors of 3 starting from the first term instead of n − 1). (b) Correct rule: uₙ = 2 × 3ⁿ⁻¹. Check: n = 1 → 2 × 3⁰ = 2 ✓; n = 2 → 2 × 3¹ = 6 ✓; n = 3 → 2 × 3² = 18 ✓; n = 4 → 2 × 3³ = 54 ✓.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for both substitutions shown (6 and 18), 1 for explaining the shift (exponent should be n − 1 / rule gives first term 6). (b) 2 marks: 1 for the correct rule uₙ = 2 × 3ⁿ⁻¹ (equivalent forms accepted), 1 for the four checks (all four terms verified).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘uₙ = 2·3ⁿ⁻¹’; checks in any order; error phrased as ‘exponent starts at n = 1 instead of 0’.","marking_criteria":"Model answer — (a) n = 1: 2 × 3¹ = 6, but the first term is 2. n = 2: 2 × 3² = 18, but the second term is 6. The student’s rule gives u₁ = 6 — it generates the sequence shifted by one term (it counts n factors of 3 starting from the first term instead of n − 1). (b) Correct rule: uₙ = 2 × 3ⁿ⁻¹. Check: n = 1 → 2 × 3⁰ = 2 ✓; n = 2 → 2 × 3¹ = 6 ✓; n = 3 → 2 × 3² = 18 ✓; n = 4 → 2 × 3³ = 54 ✓.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for both substitutions shown (6 and 18), 1 for explaining the shift (exponent should be n − 1 / rule gives first term 6). (b) 2 marks: 1 for the correct rule uₙ = 2 × 3ⁿ⁻¹ (equivalent forms accepted), 1 for the four checks (all four terms verified).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘uₙ = 2·3ⁿ⁻¹’; checks in any order; error phrased as ‘exponent starts at n = 1 instead of 0’.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"any","response_guidance":""},"marking":"Model answer — (a) n = 1: 2 × 3¹ = 6, but the first term is 2. n = 2: 2 × 3² = 18, but the second term is 6. The student’s rule gives u₁ = 6 — it generates the sequence shifted by one term (it counts n factors of 3 starting from the first term instead of n − 1). (b) Correct rule: uₙ = 2 × 3ⁿ⁻¹. Check: n = 1 → 2 × 3⁰ = 2 ✓; n = 2 → 2 × 3¹ = 6 ✓; n = 3 → 2 × 3² = 18 ✓; n = 4 → 2 × 3³ = 54 ✓.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for both substitutions shown (6 and 18), 1 for explaining the shift (exponent should be n − 1 / rule gives first term 6). (b) 2 marks: 1 for the correct rule uₙ = 2 × 3ⁿ⁻¹ (equivalent forms accepted), 1 for the four checks (all four terms verified).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘uₙ = 2·3ⁿ⁻¹’; checks in any order; error phrased as ‘exponent starts at n = 1 instead of 0’."},{"n":54,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A taxi company plans a fare rise. A journey that costs 4 500 FCFA today will cost 300 FCFA more in each following year. Write the recursive rule for the fare Fₙ in year n, where year 1 is the first year after the rise begins, and state the explicit rule.\\n\\nb Use the explicit rule to find the fare in year 10, and find how many years it takes before the fare first exceeds 10 000 FCFA. Show your working.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A taxi company plans a fare rise. A journey that costs 4 500 FCFA today will cost 300 FCFA more in each following year. Write the recursive rule for the fare Fₙ in year n, where year 1 is the first year after the rise begins, and state the explicit rule."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"b Use the explicit rule to find the fare in year 10, and find how many years it takes before the fare first exceeds 10 000 FCFA. Show your working."}]}]},"e":"Model answer — (a) Recursive: F₀ = 4500 (today’s fare), Fₙ = Fₙ₋₁ + 300 for n ≥ 1; equivalently F₁ = 4800 with Fₙ = Fₙ₋₁ + 300 from then on. Explicit: Fₙ = 4500 + 300n (equivalently 4800 + 300(n − 1)). (b) F₁₀ = 4500 + 300 × 10 = 7500 FCFA. Fare exceeds 10 000 when 4500 + 300n > 10 000 → 300n > 5500 → n > 18.33, so the first whole year is n = 19: F₁₈ = 4500 + 5400 = 9900 (not yet) and F₁₉ = 4500 + 5700 = 10 200 > 10 000 ✓. The fare will first exceed 10 000 FCFA 19 years after the rise begins.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for the recursive rule (F₀ = 4500 or equivalent with correct +300 step), 1 for the explicit rule (either form). (b) 3 marks: 1 for the year-10 fare 7500 with substitution shown, 1 for solving the inequality to n = 19 (or equivalent), 1 for the verification/meaning (F₁₈ vs F₁₉ or ‘in year 19’).\\n\\nNote on convention — the problem fixes year 1 as the first year AFTER the rise begins, so year 10 = ten years after today and Fₙ = 4500 + 300n. Any consistent convention (e.g. Fₙ = 4800 + 300(n − 1)) yields the same fare and the same answer to (b).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — Fₙ = 4800 + 300(n − 1); fare written with thousands separators; ‘19 years’.","m":5,"df":"hard","tp":"Recursive and Explicit Formulas","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":54,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"Write the rule, then solve one inequality.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a1","label":"(a) recursive rule","prompt":"Write the recursive rule for the fare Fₙ in year n, where year 1 is the first year after the rise begins.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["Today (year 0) the fare is 4500 FCFA; each following year adds 300 FCFA.","Write this year’s fare as last year’s fare plus the yearly rise, with the starting fare."],"accepted":["F₀ = 4500, Fₙ = Fₙ₋₁ + 300","F₁ = 4800, Fₙ = Fₙ₋₁ + 300","F(0) = 4500, F(n) = F(n-1) + 300","4500, each year add 300 FCFA"],"keywords":[["4500","4 500"],["300"],["+","plus","add"]],"minKeywordGroups":3},{"id":"a2","label":"(a) explicit rule","prompt":"State the explicit rule.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["Linear: start 4500, add 300 for each of the n years after the rise begins.","Turn the year-by-year rule into one equation in n."],"accepted":["Fₙ = 4500 + 300n","Fn = 4500 + 300n","F(n) = 4500 + 300n","Fₙ = 4800 + 300(n − 1)","Fn = 4800 + 300(n-1)","4500 + 300n"],"keywords":[["4500","4 500","4800","4 800"],["300"]],"minKeywordGroups":2},{"id":"b1","label":"(b) fare in year 10","prompt":"Find the fare in year 10.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Substitute n = 10 into the explicit rule.","Evaluate the explicit rule at n = 10."],"answer":7500,"tolerance":0,"unit":"FCFA"},{"id":"b2","label":"(b) years to exceed 10 000 FCFA","prompt":"Find how many years it takes before the fare first exceeds 10 000 FCFA.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Solve 4500 + 300n > 10 000.","Solve the inequality, then take the first whole year that passes."],"answer":19,"tolerance":0,"unit":"years"},{"id":"b3","label":"(b) working and meaning","prompt":"Show your working: the year-19 and year-18 fares, and what this means in context.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["Compute the fare one year before and one year after the threshold is crossed.","Say in which of those years the fare first passes 10 000 FCFA."],"accepted":["F₁₈ = 9900 and F₁₉ = 10 200, so the fare first exceeds 10 000 FCFA in year 19","year 19: F(19) = 10 200 > 10 000; F(18) = 9 900 < 10 000"],"keywords":[["9900","9 900"],["10200","10 200","10,200"],["19"]],"minKeywordGroups":2}],"marking":"Model answer — (a) Recursive: F₀ = 4500 (today’s fare), Fₙ = Fₙ₋₁ + 300 for n ≥ 1; equivalently F₁ = 4800 with Fₙ = Fₙ₋₁ + 300 from then on. Explicit: Fₙ = 4500 + 300n (equivalently 4800 + 300(n − 1)). (b) F₁₀ = 4500 + 300 × 10 = 7500 FCFA. Fare exceeds 10 000 when 4500 + 300n > 10 000 → 300n > 5500 → n > 18.33, so the first whole year is n = 19: F₁₈ = 4500 + 5400 = 9900 (not yet) and F₁₉ = 4500 + 5700 = 10 200 > 10 000 ✓. The fare will first exceed 10 000 FCFA 19 years after the rise begins.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for the recursive rule (F₀ = 4500 or equivalent with correct +300 step), 1 for the explicit rule (either form). (b) 3 marks: 1 for the year-10 fare 7500 with substitution shown, 1 for solving the inequality to n = 19 (or equivalent), 1 for the verification/meaning (F₁₈ vs F₁₉ or ‘in year 19’).\\n\\nNote on convention — the problem fixes year 1 as the first year AFTER the rise begins, so year 10 = ten years after today and Fₙ = 4500 + 300n. Any consistent convention (e.g. Fₙ = 4800 + 300(n − 1)) yields the same fare and the same answer to (b).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — Fₙ = 4800 + 300(n − 1); fare written with thousands separators; ‘19 years’.","marking_criteria":"Model answer — (a) Recursive: F₀ = 4500 (today’s fare), Fₙ = Fₙ₋₁ + 300 for n ≥ 1; equivalently F₁ = 4800 with Fₙ = Fₙ₋₁ + 300 from then on. Explicit: Fₙ = 4500 + 300n (equivalently 4800 + 300(n − 1)). (b) F₁₀ = 4500 + 300 × 10 = 7500 FCFA. Fare exceeds 10 000 when 4500 + 300n > 10 000 → 300n > 5500 → n > 18.33, so the first whole year is n = 19: F₁₈ = 4500 + 5400 = 9900 (not yet) and F₁₉ = 4500 + 5700 = 10 200 > 10 000 ✓. The fare will first exceed 10 000 FCFA 19 years after the rise begins.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for the recursive rule (F₀ = 4500 or equivalent with correct +300 step), 1 for the explicit rule (either form). (b) 3 marks: 1 for the year-10 fare 7500 with substitution shown, 1 for solving the inequality to n = 19 (or equivalent), 1 for the verification/meaning (F₁₈ vs F₁₉ or ‘in year 19’).\\n\\nNote on convention — the problem fixes year 1 as the first year AFTER the rise begins, so year 10 = ten years after today and Fₙ = 4500 + 300n. Any consistent convention (e.g. Fₙ = 4800 + 300(n − 1)) yields the same fare and the same answer to (b).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — Fₙ = 4800 + 300(n − 1); fare written with thousands separators; ‘19 years’.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"any","response_guidance":""},"marking":"Model answer — (a) Recursive: F₀ = 4500 (today’s fare), Fₙ = Fₙ₋₁ + 300 for n ≥ 1; equivalently F₁ = 4800 with Fₙ = Fₙ₋₁ + 300 from then on. Explicit: Fₙ = 4500 + 300n (equivalently 4800 + 300(n − 1)). (b) F₁₀ = 4500 + 300 × 10 = 7500 FCFA. Fare exceeds 10 000 when 4500 + 300n > 10 000 → 300n > 5500 → n > 18.33, so the first whole year is n = 19: F₁₈ = 4500 + 5400 = 9900 (not yet) and F₁₉ = 4500 + 5700 = 10 200 > 10 000 ✓. The fare will first exceed 10 000 FCFA 19 years after the rise begins.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for the recursive rule (F₀ = 4500 or equivalent with correct +300 step), 1 for the explicit rule (either form). (b) 3 marks: 1 for the year-10 fare 7500 with substitution shown, 1 for solving the inequality to n = 19 (or equivalent), 1 for the verification/meaning (F₁₈ vs F₁₉ or ‘in year 19’).\\n\\nNote on convention — the problem fixes year 1 as the first year AFTER the rise begins, so year 10 = ten years after today and Fₙ = 4500 + 300n. Any consistent convention (e.g. Fₙ = 4800 + 300(n − 1)) yields the same fare and the same answer to (b).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — Fₙ = 4800 + 300(n − 1); fare written with thousands separators; ‘19 years’."},{"n":55,"t":"assess_parts","p":"The nth term of a linear sequence is uₙ = 6n − 5. Write down the first four terms of the sequence.\\n\\nb Which term of the sequence is equal to 97? Show the equation you solve.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The nth term of a linear sequence is uₙ = 6n − 5. Write down the first four terms of the sequence."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"b Which term of the sequence is equal to 97? Show the equation you solve."}]}]},"e":"Model answer — (a) u₁ = 6(1) − 5 = 1; u₂ = 7; u₃ = 13; u₄ = 19. (b) 6n − 5 = 97 → 6n = 102 → n = 17. The 17th term is equal to 97 (check: u₁₇ = 6 × 17 − 5 = 102 − 5 = 97 ✓).\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for all four terms correct (award 1 for three correct). (b) 2 marks: 1 for the equation 6n − 5 = 97 (or equivalent), 1 for n = 17 (‘the 17th term’).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘17’ / ‘17th’ / ‘n = 17’; terms with any separators.","m":4,"df":"easy","tp":"Linear Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":55,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"n = 1, 2, 3, 4 into 6n − 5; then 6n − 5 = 97.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a) first four terms","prompt":"Write down the first four terms of the sequence.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Substitute n = 1, 2, 3, 4 into uₙ = 6